  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "audience",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Who it's for",
      "html": "Consoless turns a prompt such as <em>“a falling-block puzzle on a green handheld screen”</em> into a game you can play in the browser, then keeps editing it by chat. It is for non-coders with game ideas, hobbyist developers who take over the code, classrooms, retro fans and open-source contributors. Stage 1 is retro and 2D games, Stage 2 adds 3D, and Stage 3 adds desktop installers."
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "The whole system",
      "legend": true,
      "caption": "<strong>Read it top to bottom.</strong> The browser reaches <code>api/common</code> through the web server's <code>/api/v1</code> proxy. Go owns Postgres, Redis and every durable object. Below the Kafka bar, <code>api/generate</code> calls the model and <code>api/verify</code> runs each candidate headlessly; both report back for Go to save. Games load only in a sandboxed iframe from the play CDN, never from the app's own origin. The dotted grey line is telemetry.",
      "diagram": {
        "width": 1200,
        "height": 700,
        "aria": "Consoless architecture. The browser loads the web app and calls /api/v1, which the web server proxies to api/common (Go, CRUD). The browser embeds a sandboxed game iframe that loads bundles from the play CDN, which reads the dedicated play bucket using signed requests. api/common owns Postgres and Redis, writes to Cloud Storage and promotes bundles to the play bucket. api/common, api/intake (Stage 2), api/generate, api/verify and api/build (Stage 3) exchange events over the shared Kafka bus. api/generate calls Vertex AI. Processing services read and write the compute and tmp prefixes in Cloud Storage. Every service sends telemetry to the observability gateway.",
